WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.298
By the shop door stands an apple dispenser.

2
00:00:05.298 --> 00:00:07.623
Pull the lever, and an apple drops out.

3
00:00:07.623 --> 00:00:13.467
A customer who wants some fruit can use it happily, because every apple that drops out is a fruit.

4
00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:17.224
There's no slot to put anything in, and that's the whole point.

5
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:25.888
Last lesson, a crate of apples couldn't pass as a crate of fruit, because a crate of fruit takes any fruit, so a lemon could go in.

6
00:00:25.888 --> 00:00:27.960
The dispenser has no way in at all.

7
00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:32.094
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:32.094 --> 00:00:33.741
My dispenser has no slot.

9
00:00:33.741 --> 00:00:39.143
Why does the compiler still say a dispenser of apples is not a dispenser of fruit?

10
00:00:39.143 --> 00:00:41.053
Can't it see there's no slot?

11
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:44.457
Here's the dispenser in Kotlin.

12
00:00:44.457 --> 00:00:48.503
next hands out the next item, or null when it's empty.

13
00:00:48.503 --> 00:00:53.075
totalGrams wants a Dispenser&lt;Fruit&gt;, and adds up the weights.

14
00:00:53.075 --> 00:00:58.096
Our dispenser holds two apples: Gala at 180 grams, and Fuji at 150.

15
00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:02.286
Hand it the apples, and Kotlin says: argument type mismatch.

16
00:01:02.286 --> 00:01:06.775
Actual type is Dispenser&lt;Apple&gt;, but Dispenser&lt;Fruit&gt; was expected.

17
00:01:06.775 --> 00:01:10.124
The compiler can't see the missing slot by itself.

18
00:01:10.124 --> 00:01:13.675
Generic types are invariant unless you say otherwise.

19
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:17.915
So let's run the substitution test from last lesson.

20
00:01:17.915 --> 00:01:23.397
Some code expects a dispenser of fruit, and we secretly hand it a dispenser of apples.

21
00:01:23.397 --> 00:01:25.310
What can that code do with it?

22
00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:26.712
Can anything go wrong?

23
00:01:26.712 --> 00:01:27.732
Pause and guess.

24
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:32.771
All it can do is pull the lever.

25
00:01:32.771 --> 00:01:36.846
Every call to next returns an apple, and every apple is a fruit.

26
00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:40.858
No move takes a fruit in, so there's nowhere for a lemon to go.

27
00:01:40.858 --> 00:01:42.195
Nothing can go wrong.

28
00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:48.368
So we tell the compiler, once, on the declaration, with one small word: out.

29
00:01:48.368 --> 00:01:49.554
Dispenser&lt;out T&gt;.

30
00:01:49.554 --> 00:01:53.391
It's a promise: values of T only come out of this type.

31
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:55.056
Same call as before.

32
00:01:55.056 --> 00:01:56.655
Nothing else changed.

33
00:01:56.655 --> 00:01:58.102
What does it print?

34
00:01:58.102 --> 00:01:59.320
Pause and guess.

35
00:02:02.333 --> 00:02:08.558
It compiles, and the lesson's ProducerDemo prints: apple total, 330 grams.

36
00:02:08.558 --> 00:02:13.184
That's 180 plus 150, read through a dispenser of fruit.

37
00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:14.962
Let's map it.

38
00:02:14.962 --> 00:02:18.820
The lever is a return type: a T flows out to the caller.

39
00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:21.851
A slot would be a parameter: a T flowing in.

40
00:02:21.851 --> 00:02:26.467
And out is a sticker on the machine that says: lever only, no slot.

41
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.105
I know what you're thinking.

42
00:02:29.105 --> 00:02:31.784
What stops someone from adding a slot later?

43
00:02:31.784 --> 00:02:34.097
Say, a refill function that takes a T.

44
00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:35.417
Follow it through.

45
00:02:35.417 --> 00:02:38.486
A Dispenser&lt;Apple&gt; passes as a Dispenser&lt;Fruit&gt;.

46
00:02:38.486 --> 00:02:43.537
Through that view, refill with a lemon type checks, because a lemon is a fruit.

47
00:02:43.537 --> 00:02:49.675
Now the apple dispenser holds a lemon, and the next pull hands it to code that expects an apple.

48
00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:55.150
An apple dispenser with a slot for any fruit isn't an apple dispenser any more.

49
00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.755
It's a surprise machine.

50
00:02:56.755 --> 00:03:05.716
So the compiler checks every member that outside code can call, and refuses the slot: T is declared out, but occurs in an in position.

51
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:07.467
Now the name.

52
00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:08.734
This is covariance.

53
00:03:08.734 --> 00:03:16.537
Co means together: the subtype arrow between the element types carries over to the dispensers, in the same direction.

54
00:03:16.537 --> 00:03:22.005
Apple is a subtype of Fruit, so Dispenser&lt;Apple&gt; is a subtype of Dispenser&lt;Fruit&gt;.

55
00:03:22.005 --> 00:03:25.940
And a type that only hands values out is called a producer.

56
00:03:26.867 --> 00:03:29.223
Java has no out on a declaration.

57
00:03:29.223 --> 00:03:38.006
Its Dispenser stays invariant, so every method that wants the flexible behavior says so where it's used, with a wildcard: ?

58
00:03:38.006 --> 00:03:41.219
extends Fruit, meaning some subtype of Fruit.

59
00:03:41.219 --> 00:03:47.217
Forget it, and javac says: Dispenser&lt;Apple&gt; cannot be converted to Dispenser&lt;Fruit&gt;.

60
00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:50.863
That's the difference between the two styles.

61
00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:53.592
Kotlin puts the sticker on the machine, once.

62
00:03:53.592 --> 00:03:59.656
Java hands every customer a note to carry in their pocket, and anyone who forgets it is turned away.

63
00:03:59.656 --> 00:04:03.477
The names are declaration-site variance, and use-site variance.

64
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.532
And the guardrail.

65
00:04:05.532 --> 00:04:12.260
You can only make the out promise if it's true: T may appear only where values come out, like return types.

66
00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:15.153
And out doesn't mean read-only, or unchanging.

67
00:04:15.153 --> 00:04:18.359
Every pull still takes an apple out of the machine.

68
00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:20.497
The end of this lesson shows that.

69
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.412
So, why couldn't the compiler see there's no slot?

70
00:04:24.412 --> 00:04:27.726
Because it trusts what you declare, not what you meant.

71
00:04:27.726 --> 00:04:32.727
Say out, and an apple dispenser is a fruit dispenser, because only apples come out.

72
00:04:32.867 --> 00:04:33.756
Quick check.

73
00:04:33.756 --> 00:04:38.128
Inside Dispenser&lt;out T&gt;, which of these two lines compiles?

74
00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:42.466
Only val latest.

75
00:04:42.466 --> 00:04:45.653
A val only has a getter, so T only comes out.

76
00:04:45.653 --> 00:04:53.300
A var also has a setter, a slot, so T would go in too: the compiler says it occurs in an invariant position.
